  "kind": "implicit_derivative",
  "title": "Implicit differentiation",
  "prompt": "The curve $x^2 + xy + y^2 = 7$ defines $y$ implicitly as a function of $x$. Find $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$.",
  "answer_format": "An expression in x and y.",
  "difficulty": 2,
  "tags": [
    "implicit differentiation"
  ],
  "data": {
    "equation": "x^2 + x y + y^2 = 7"
  },
  "answer": "-(2x + y)/(x + 2y)",
  "mistakes": [],
  "hints": [
    "Differentiate both sides with respect to x. The term xy needs the product rule.",
    "Collect the dy/dx terms on one side and factor dy/dx out."
  ],
  "solution": [
    "$2x + \\left(y + x\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right) + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} = 0$.",
    "$(x + 2y)\\frac{dy}{dx} = -(2x + y)$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\frac{2x + y}{x + 2y}$."
  ],
